fix(pip): Make the lens sampling mapping identity so the lens cannot go black

Review of the previous change, which is where this ends up: the corrected mapping was still a
guess at an unknown quantity.

What is known: the lens texture already holds the magnified view of the optic, and the shader
pastes the sampled patch across the whole aperture. What is not known to the engine is how much
of the screen the optic's aperture covers, which is what the patch has to line up with, and every
attempt to derive the mapping from the camera matrices measured wrong - spans of 1.5 and 0.62
first, then 0.0 in both conventions, the latter being a degenerate map that shows a single texel
stretched over the aperture: a lens that is black whatever direction it points, which is what the
player reported.

Identity cannot be degenerate, so the published mapping is identity: the aperture shows the lens
texture as it is, which is a magnified image and visible. The aperture relative scale is the
remaining refinement and it needs an input the engine does not have yet - the reference computes
it from the optic's geometry (svp_optics: 2 * ocular radius / eye distance, and the applied disc
size), so that is the next piece of work rather than another camera matrix experiment.

Audit of the same class while here: checked every set_Element(...->E[x]) in the render layer for
another out of range index. All the others are fixed indices into blenders that have those
elements (the SSFX blur/SSR/SSS families); s_heatvision->E[ps_r2_heatvision] was the only one
indexed by a console variable, and it is fixed now.